window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"t":"Cat-GDI8 documentation","u":"index.html","x":"Level 1. Introduction The control system and injection layouts. Basic knowledge of engines is assumed. What an ECU is The control loop: sensors, model, actuators Injection types PFI, GDI and hybrid Engine layouts Configurator: what your engine needs Level 2. How it works How the unit thinks and makes decisions. Torque-based control How the lever becomes air and spark Air and fuel Filling, mixture, injection, pressure, cams Rev limiter and propeller Protecting the engine when the propeller unloads Engine start From key-on to idle Protections and reactions What the unit does on a fault One unit, different engines What is described as data Level 3. Build and set up What to do, in order, on your engine. Step-by-step build From preparation to first start and tuning Working with calibrations Maps, axes, saving, files. Tools Calculators and live diagrams Level 4. Modules in detail Every component, as in the application. Described in detail: 7 of 80 sections. Engine Cylinders, displacement, installed components sections: 3 Trigger How the unit finds shaft position sections: 3 Sensors Temperatures, pressures, lever, lambda sections: 16 Actuators Injectors, coils, HP pump, throttle, valves s"},{"t":"What an ECU is","u":"learn/ecu.html","x":"The control loop The ECU works in a closed loop: it measures the engine state, calculates cylinder filling, fuel mass and spark timing, and commands the actuators. The result is measured again, so the unit compensates for temperature, pressure, wear and part-to-part variation. Sensors crank and cam angle, MAP, temperatures, rail pressure, lever, lambda, knock ↓ Model in the ECU torque, air, fuel mass, injection timing, spark timing ↓ Actuators injectors, coils, throttle, high-pressure pump, wastegate, cam phasers ↓ Engine its response returns through the sensors Time scale In a four-stroke engine each cylinder fires once every two revolutions. That sets the time the unit has to prepare the next injection and spark. Engine speed Cylinders Firings per second - Between firings - What Cat-GDI8 is An ECU for gasoline engines of up to 8 cylinders with direct injection (GDI), port injection (PFI) or a hybrid layout, with torque-based control, electronic throttle, boost and cam phasers. The whole unit On the left what the unit sees, in the middle what it decides (everything in torque), on the right what it drives. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for"},{"t":"Injection types","u":"learn/injection.html","x":"Injection layouts Layouts differ in where fuel is delivered, at what pressure and when in the cycle. Port (PFI) Direct (GDI) Hybrid Injector in the intake port Pressure: typically 3–6 bar. Fuel is delivered into the intake port ahead of the valve, usually before the intake stroke. The mixture has time to prepare, but part of the fuel forms a film on the port walls. Pros: simple fuel system, good homogenization, fuel washes the valves. Cons: transport delay from the wall film, less charge cooling. Injector in the cylinder Pressure: typically 50–350 bar. Fuel is delivered straight into the combustion chamber. Pressure comes from a cam-driven high-pressure pump; the injector is opened by a driver with a high-voltage current peak and a hold phase. Pros: precise fuel per cycle, multiple injections, charge cooling and a larger knock margin. Cons: complex driver and pump, carbon build-up on intake valves, limited mixing time at high rpm. Two injectors per cylinder Pressure: each injector has its own. For each cylinder the ECU splits the fuel per cycle between the GDI and PFI injectors depending on the operating point. Pros: extra flow capacity, clean valves, flexibility across operating p"},{"t":"Engine layouts","u":"learn/schemes.html","x":"Engine configurator Choose a configuration to see the required components and the application modules where they are set up. Injection Direct (GDI) Port (PFI) Hybrid Air Naturally aspirated One turbo Two turbos Throttles One Two Cam phasers None Intake only Intake and exhaust Electronic throttle and torque request There is no mechanical link between the lever and the throttle. Lever position sets a torque request ; the coordinator takes idle control and limits into account, then the model calculates the required filling and throttle position. This is why the rev limiter and protections act regardless of lever position. Load source: MAP or throttle MAP sensor Throttle Speed-density. Filling is calculated from intake pressure, rpm, temperature and cam positions. The main mode; it also works with boost. Alpha-N. Load is taken from throttle angle and rpm. Used when MAP fails and on engines with unstable vacuum, for example with individual throttle bodies."},{"t":"Torque-based control","u":"how/torque.html","x":"Torque-based control In progress The unit does not convert the throttle lever directly into throttle opening. The lever is a torque request . The model decides how to deliver this torque — with air and spark timing — and all limits act on torque. This is the same principle as in modern OEM engine management, adapted to an airboat: engine, gearbox and a heavy propeller. Lever torque request from lever position and rpm Idle controller keeps idle speed, holds a torque reserve ↓ Coordination highest request wins, then every limit is applied Limits rev limiter, protections, start, limp modes — limits always win ↓ Torque target request + friction and pumping losses ↓ Air path · slow cylinder filling → intake pressure → throttle angle, cam targets, boost Spark path · fast spark retard from optimum for quick torque changes Emergency cut separate setting, off by default Model levels Position and time — crankshaft and four camshafts give the engine angle and cam phases. Sensors — every value has a validity flag; a failed sensor is replaced by a substitute or model value. Engine model — cylinder filling, residual gas, spark and mixture efficiency, losses, exhaust temperature. Torque structure"},{"t":"Air and fuel","u":"how/air-fuel.html","x":"Air and fuel In progress Cylinder filling Air mass is calculated from intake pressure with a linear model: slope and offset depend on rpm and on intake and exhaust cam positions, with a temperature correction. Each bank is calculated separately because each has its own cam phases. The model is easy to invert: from the required air mass the unit gets the required intake pressure and, through the throttle flow model, the throttle angle. Fuel Fuel mass per cylinder = air mass / (stoichiometric ratio × target lambda). Target lambda comes from the base map, start and warm-up corrections, component protection enrichment and the wideband correction. Hybrid injection For each cylinder the calculated fuel is split between the direct injector and the port injector. Direct injection can use 1–3 pulses per cycle with a high-voltage current profile from the calibration; start of injection depends on rpm and load. Injector open time is calculated from the injector model for the actual pressure difference. Rail pressure Two high-pressure pumps, normally one per bank. Target pressure is a 32×32 map of rpm and load; control combines feed-forward from fuel demand with a rail model and integral actio"},{"t":"Rev limiter and propeller","u":"how/limiter.html","x":"Rev limiter and propeller protection In progress An unloaded propeller can over-rev the engine almost instantly, so the limiter works ahead: it takes shaft acceleration into account and removes torque smoothly in a band below the limit, quickly with spark, then with air. Emergency fuel cut on extreme over-rev is a separate setting and is off by default. In progress Torque rises through a soft zone that takes up driveline backlash, then faster. Rise and fall rates are curves over rpm. Planned A drive model (engine, gearbox, propeller), damping of torsional oscillations with spark timing, and a keep-out band around the driveline resonance."},{"t":"Engine start","u":"how/start.html","x":"Engine start In progress The strategy counts events (injection number, engine cycles) rather than seconds. Everything below is implemented in code and tested on the model; it has not yet been verified on a running engine. Phase What happens Status Prime Lift pumps run, the unit waits for cranking. In progress Quick start Crank position is found from the camshaft shape before the gap; the gap then confirms it. In progress Waiting for pressure No injection until the rail reaches the first-injection pressure. In progress High pressure Injection on the compression stroke. In progress Low-pressure fallback If pressure is not reached within a set number of revolutions, injection moves to the intake stroke with a long pulse. In progress Start dose Base dose in mg by temperature, corrected by injection number, pressure and rpm. In progress Flood clear Lever fully open while cranking: no fuel is injected. In progress Repeat and hot start Separate dose corrections. In progress After start Extra fuel fades over engine cycles, raised idle, hand-over to the torque model. In progress Warm-up Lambda correction by coolant temperature. In progress Adaptation Basic learning of the start dose. In pro"},{"t":"Protections and reactions","u":"how/protection.html","x":"Protections and fault reactions In progress The main requirement: the boat must get back to shore . Every fault has a code in OBD format and a reaction level. If several are active, the strictest applies. Torque is reduced smoothly, without cutting fuel or spark. Level Reaction Examples R0 Fault code only Knock sensor noise, wideband sensor R1 Torque and filling limit MAP sensor replaced by model R2 Limp mode: as R1 plus an rpm limit (~2500) to get home Throttle not responding, both lever channels or rail sensor failed R3 Engine stop Not assigned to any fault by default Coolant, oil, exhaust and gearbox temperatures and oil pressure reduce torque in four steps. The lowest step is a “get home” mode, not zero. Injection and spark are timed by hardware timers. A silent second core is detected as a fault. An external power-management chip with a watchdog supervises the processor. Stepped protections Each cause gives only a torque fraction from 0 to 1, and the strictest applies. The lowest step is a get-home mode, not zero. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic."},{"t":"One unit, different engines","u":"how/principle.html","x":"One unit, different engines Ready Cat-GDI8 is not built for one specific engine. Everything that depends on the engine and its wiring is described as data in BrainBus GDI-8 and stored in the unit. The firmware is the same for every installation. The control model works with logical values — intake pressure, intake cam position of bank A, the phaser valve of bank A. The configuration decides where each value comes from and which physical pin each output uses. The application shows which pins are suitable for each function and checks the result: one pin — one function. Up to 8 cylinders, firing order and cylinder angles (even and uneven V8, V6…), bank of each cylinder. Injectors: flow at reference pressure, opening delay from voltage and pressure, minimum pulse. Coils: passive 12 V or with built-in igniter 5 V; dwell from battery voltage. 0–4 camshafts with roles (intake/exhaust, bank A/B), each with its own wheel and phaser. Boost: none, one or two turbos; wastegate pneumatic or electric; 1 or 2 throttles. Knock sensors 0–2, wideband lambda 0–2, propeller drive parameters if known."},{"t":"How to read diagrams","u":"how/diagrams.html","x":"Diagram elements A blue oval is an input, a teal block is a curve or map, white blocks are arithmetic, purple is logic, orange is dynamics and a green oval is an output. Click to enlarge. Icons A thermometer is temperature, a gauge is pressure, a gear is rpm, a plug is spark, an injector is fuel, a shield is protection. Click to enlarge."},{"t":"Step-by-step build","u":"build/index.html","x":"Important: the firmware is in development and this guide has not yet been verified on a running engine. Do not skip stages. 1. Choose your engine The guide shows only the steps you need. Your choice is remembered in the browser. Injection Direct (GDI) Port (PFI) Hybrid Air Naturally aspirated One turbo Two turbos Throttles One Two Cam phasers None Intake only Intake and exhaust 2. Follow the stages 1. Preparation and safety A first start always carries risk: backfire, fuel leak, starter shock. 2. Connect the ECU Make sure the unit is alive and sees itself before touching any actuator. 3. Describe the engine The unit is universal, so first tell it what engine you have. 4. Check the sensors With wrong sensors the unit calculates the wrong dose and the wrong angle. 5. Check the actuators Each output must fire where intended. Engine stopped, no fuel. 6. Set up the trigger The foundation of everything. Cranking without fuel and without spark. 7. TDC and spark The unit must know where top dead center of cylinder 1 is. Spark on, no fuel. 8. Base maps The first start runs on numbers known to be safe. 9. First start Short attempts, with a log review after each one. 10. Finish the tune The e"},{"t":"1. Preparation and safety","u":"build/01-safety.html","x":"You will need Fire extinguisher Ventilation Charged battery and charger Multimeter Spark plug wrench Steps Remove fire risks Extinguisher at hand, room ventilated, no fuel leaks. Fill the fluids Oil and coolant at the correct level. Check the battery 12.5–14.5 V with no load. While cranking the voltage must not fall below 10 V. Remove the spark plugs Up to and including the trigger stage the engine turns without compression. Put the plugs in the coils and rest them on engine ground. Done when Everything on the list is in place Plugs are resting on engine ground Stop if There is a fuel smell or leak The battery does not hold voltage Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"2. Connect the ECU","u":"build/02-connect.html","x":"You will need Windows PC Ethernet cable BrainBus GDI-8 application Actuators → Power control → Switches Steps Apply power Switch the ignition on. Do not use the starter. Connect to the PC Plug in the Ethernet cable and press “Connect”. Compare versions Check the firmware on the “ECU” page. The application and firmware must be the same version. Check power In the power module, the switches are on and there are no faults. Done when The application shows “Connected” Prime mode, 0 rpm No power or injector driver faults Stop if Power or driver faults: do not continue Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"3. Describe the engine","u":"build/03-describe.html","x":"You will need Engine specifications Firing order Harness diagram and pinout Engine → Configuration → Engine Steps Fill in the engine Cylinders, displacement, firing order, banks. Choose GDI injectors Flow at reference pressure and opening delay. Choose PFI injectors Flow and opening delay. Describe boost Turbos and wastegate type. Describe the camshafts Cam roles and phasers. Assign pins Each function gets its own pin, as in your harness. Save to ECU Structural changes are applied with the engine stopped. Done when Only the modules you need appear in the application No pin conflicts Stop if You do not know the firing order or wiring: find out first Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"4. Check the sensors","u":"build/04-sensors.html","x":"You will need Multimeter Sensor datasheets Sensors → Coolant temperature → Calibration & raw Steps Compare readings On a cold engine coolant and air are close to ambient, MAP is close to barometric. Check the rail With no pump running, pressure is near zero. Unplug the connector A fault appears in the sensor errors and the value goes to substitute. Plug it back The fault clears. Verify the calibration Enter the values from the sensor datasheet. Do not guess. Done when Every sensor reads plausibly An unplugged connector shows as a fault Stop if Crankshaft sensor not working Rail sensor not working on a GDI engine Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"5. Check the actuators","u":"build/05-actuators.html","x":"You will need Plugs in coils, resting on ground A helper to watch and listen Actuators → Ignition coils → Outputs Steps Lift pumps Run the test: the pump hums and feed pressure rises. Then switch it off. Coils one by one Which plug sparks? Compare with the cylinder. Never fire a coil without a plug on ground. GDI injectors Rail without pressure, pumps stopped. Click each one and watch the opening current. High-pressure pump valves Listen for the click. PFI injectors Rail without pressure. Click each one. Throttle Check sensors, end stops and travel. Done when Every output fired in its own cylinder Stop if An output does not fire or hits the wrong cylinder: fix the wiring or assignment Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"6. Set up the trigger","u":"build/06-trigger.html","x":"You will need Pump relays or fuses removed Ignition coils supply switched off Charged battery Trigger → Wheel → Wheel Steps Remove fuel Press the fuel-off button on Trigger → Wheel and remove the pump relays. These are two independent barriers. Remove spark Switch off “Ignition coils supply”. Do not switch off the GDI supply for this. Check the signal “Signal” logger, starter for 3–5 s: crank edges are present. Identify the wheel Record 4 s of cranking, then “Write to ECU”. Check stability “Online”, 5–10 s: sync appears within 1–2 revolutions and is not lost. Set up the camshafts Record the cam shapes and write them to the ECU. Check with plugs in Fit the plugs. Still no fuel and no spark. Sync must not be lost on compression. Done when Wheel identified 720° cycle sync is stable Zero injector pulses in the log Stop if Injector pulses in the log Sync is being lost Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"7. TDC and spark","u":"build/07-tdc.html","x":"You will need Timing light TDC mark on the pulley Trigger → Wheel → TDC Steps Find TDC TDC tab: record while cranking, then “Write to ECU”. Enable spark Switch on “Ignition coils supply”. The fuel-off button stays on, pumps stay removed. Check with a timing light While cranking the mark must sit at the start angle. Correct it with the “Timing light” buttons. Check every cylinder Timing light on each cylinder's lead: same mark, correct firing order. Done when The mark matches the start angle Sparks follow the firing order Stop if Difference over 2°: repeat and find the cause Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"8. Base maps","u":"build/08-maps.html","x":"You will need Injector data Displacement and compression ratio Start → Start fuel → Fuel Steps Start angle A small advance, around 5–10° before TDC. Never after TDC, or the engine may run backwards. Running angles Conservative, without large advance. Start fuel Dose by coolant temperature. Start pressure First-injection pressure and the low-pressure fallback. Save to ECU Otherwise the settings are lost at power-off. Done when Start angle is before TDC Settings are saved Stop if If in doubt about the angle: do not start Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"9. First start","u":"build/09-firststart.html","x":"You will need Fire extinguisher A helper Log recording Start → Now → Start state Steps Put everything back Plugs, coils, injectors, pump relays. Release the fuel-off button. Start recording Rpm, sync, pressure, battery, coolant. Starter up to 5 s Watch: pressure builds, injections run, rpm rises. Did not start Stop and review the log. If the plugs are flooded: flood clear, lever fully open while cranking. Started Do not rev. Check oil pressure, leaks and temperatures at once. Shut down after 30–60 s and review the log. Done when Engine holds rpm Oil pressure present No leaks Stop if No oil pressure: shut down immediately Fuel leak Backfire Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"10. Finish the tune","u":"build/10-tune.html","x":"You will need Wideband lambda Log recording Fuel & mixture → Lambda target → Maps Steps Idle Speed and torque reserve on a warm engine. Mixture Compare lambda with the target and correct the filling. Ignition Increase angles gradually while watching for knock. Boost Raise in steps, only after the mixture is set. Cam timing Enable after checking oil pressure. Load Smoothly, from light to heavy. Done when Lambda close to target No knock Temperatures normal Stop if Knock Lean mixture under load Overheating Want to know why: how it works ."},{"t":"Working with calibrations","u":"setup/calibration.html","x":"Working with calibrations Ready Maps are 32×32, curves have 16 points. Each value is checked by the unit; a value outside limits is refused. Table axes are shared: change the rpm axis once and every table with this axis is recalculated, keeping the same values at the same rpm. Pressure axes follow the calibration of their sensors. Structural changes — wheels, output assignment, number of cylinders — are applied only with the engine stopped. Calibration values can be changed while the engine runs. A calibration can be saved to and loaded from a file on the PC. Questions about the documentation: info@ailuohai-tech.com ."},{"t":"Engine","u":"modules/engine.html","x":"Module sections Configuration Tabs: Engine, Model, Errors & limits Description coming ECU equipment Tabs: Channels Description coming Table axes Tabs: Axes Description coming"},{"t":"Configuration","u":"modules/engine/config.html","x":"What is in the application Engine → Configuration Engine Live view in the application. Model 3 Engine displacement Segment lead Stoichiometric AFR Errors & limits Planned Configuration check: channels, firing order, sensors."},{"t":"ECU equipment","u":"modules/engine/caps.html","x":"What is in the application Engine → ECU equipment Channels Live view in the application."},{"t":"Table axes","u":"modules/engine/axes.html","x":"What is in the application Engine → Table axes Axes Live view in the application."},{"t":"Trigger","u":"modules/trigger.html","x":"Module sections Inputs Tabs: Sensors, Errors Description coming Wheel Tabs: Wheel, Crankshaft, Camshafts, TDC, Adaptation Description coming Logger Tabs: Signal Description coming"},{"t":"Inputs","u":"modules/trigger/inputs.html","x":"What is in the application Trigger → Inputs Sensors Crankshaft VR / Hall, camshafts 1..4 on DIGITAL_1..6 Errors Configuration check and ECU counters: sync loss, thresholds, camshafts, noise"},{"t":"Wheel","u":"modules/trigger/wheel.html","x":"What is in the application Trigger → Wheel Wheel 1 Crankshaft and camshaft wheels: description and picture Segment lead Crankshaft Steps: without plugs (wheel, sync point, thresholds) -> with all plugs (thresholds widened for compressions) Camshafts Cranking without plugs: both edges of every camshaft on the wheel - shape of the intake and exhaust wheels TDC One plug in: TDC of cylinder 1 in the cycle (phase by the main camshaft); offset, timing light, loss angle Adaptation Planned Live view in the application."},{"t":"Logger","u":"modules/trigger/log.html","x":"What is in the application Trigger → Logger Signal 19 Every edge of every input: raw (rejected by the ECU filters - by reason) and after the filters; filter settings - on the left, under the sections Input capture filter Crank dead time Crank spark blanking Crank sensor delay Cam dead time Cam spark blanking Cam sensor delay Level check (DIGITAL_4..6) VR: variant A VR: filter VR: auto hysteresis VR: hysteresis VR: falling edge filter VR: hysteresis after filter VR: double edge VR: force minimum hysteresis VR: start hysteresis VR: teeth before run setting VR: sensor check"},{"t":"Sensors","u":"modules/sensors.html","x":"Module sections Coolant temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Described Intake air temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Oil temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Gearbox temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Fuel temperature Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Fuel level Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Manifold pressure / boost Tabs: Calibration & raw, Crank-synchronous, Errors & limits Description coming Barometer Tabs: Readings, Errors & limits Description coming Oil pressure Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Fuel pressure, low Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Fuel rail pressure Tabs: Rails, Sensor A, Sensor B, Crank-synchronous, Errors A, Errors B Description coming Lever / pedal Tabs: Calibration & raw, Errors & limits Description coming Lambda Tabs: Readings, Details, Sensors & heating, Module CAN, Errors & limits Description coming Exhaust temperature (EGT) Tabs: Readings, Details, Module CAN, Errors & limits Description coming Knock sensors Tabs: Sensors &"},{"t":"Coolant temperature","u":"modules/sensors/clt.html","x":"Purpose The coolant temperature sensor (CLT) reports the thermal state of the engine. It is installed in the cooling jacket, usually near the thermostat. The unit uses this temperature to choose the start dose, warm-up enrichment and idle speed, and limits torque in steps when the engine overheats. Important: the sensor is passive. The unit calculates temperature from its resistance, so the calibration must match the specific sensor. How it works The sensing element is an NTC thermistor: resistance falls as temperature rises. The sensor is connected to ground and forms a voltage divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V. Temperature the sensing element heats up ↓ Resistance falls along the NTC curve ↓ Input voltage divider with the reference resistor ↓ The unit calculates degrees using the sensor calibration Sensor characteristic Temperature Resistance at 25 °C β coefficient Sensor resistance - Input voltage - State - Example for a typical sensor. Take exact values from your sensor's datasheet. Wiring The sensor has two wires with no polarity. One wire goes to the ECU input. By default this is IO-5 , pin J1.26. The other goes to the ECU sensor ground , not to the chassis or "},{"t":"Intake air temperature","u":"modules/sensors/iat.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Intake air temperature Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Oil temperature","u":"modules/sensors/oilT.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Oil temperature Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Gearbox temperature","u":"modules/sensors/gearT.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Gearbox temperature Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Fuel temperature","u":"modules/sensors/fuelT.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Fuel temperature Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Fuel level","u":"modules/sensors/fuelLvl.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Fuel level Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Manifold pressure / boost","u":"modules/sensors/map.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Manifold pressure / boost Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Crank-synchronous 4 MAP first window start MAP window width MAP windows per cycle MAP prediction limit Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Barometer","u":"modules/sensors/baro.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Barometer Readings Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Oil pressure","u":"modules/sensors/oilP.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Oil pressure Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Fuel pressure, low","u":"modules/sensors/fuelLowP.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Fuel pressure, low Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Fuel rail pressure","u":"modules/sensors/rail.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Fuel rail pressure Rails One rail - sensor A only (both heads, all pumps); two rails - sensors A and B. Sensor A Live view in the application. Sensor B Live view in the application. Crank-synchronous 6 Rail A first window start Rail A window width Rail A windows per cycle Rail B first window start Rail B window width Rail B windows per cycle Errors A Live view in the application. Errors B Live view in the application."},{"t":"Lever / pedal","u":"modules/sensors/lever.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Lever / pedal Calibration & raw Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Lambda","u":"modules/sensors/lambda.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Lambda Readings Live view in the application. Details Live view in the application. Sensors & heating Live view in the application. Module CAN Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Exhaust temperature (EGT)","u":"modules/sensors/egt.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Exhaust temperature (EGT) Readings Live view in the application. Details Live view in the application. Module CAN Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Knock sensors","u":"modules/sensors/knock.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Knock sensors Sensors & signal Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Voltages","u":"modules/sensors/volt.html","x":"What is in the application Sensors → Voltages Readings Live view in the application. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Actuators","u":"modules/actuators.html","x":"Rail pressure Target from a map, feed-forward from injector demand, P from a rail model and I near the target. Volume per stroke is converted to valve closing time using the cam profile. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections GDI injectors Tabs: Injector, Curves, Logger, Outputs, Errors & limits Description coming PFI injectors Tabs: Injector, Curves, Outputs, Errors & limits Description coming Ignition coils Tabs: Dwell, Current and spark, Outputs, Errors & limits Description coming HPFP Tabs: Valve, Mechanics, Curves, Controller, Pumps, Test, Errors & limits Description coming Electronic throttle Tabs: Throttle body 1, Throttle body 2, Spring 1, Spring 2, Stopped engine, Test 1, Test 2, Errors 1, Errors 2 Description coming Wastegate pneumatic Tabs: Valve A, Valve B Description coming Wastegate servo Tabs: Servo A, Servo B, Spring A, Spring B, Stopped engine, Test A, Test B, Errors A, Errors B Description coming Cam phasers Tabs: Intake A, Intake B, Exhaust A, Exhaust B, Intake regulator, Exhaust regulator, Holding (intake), Holding (exhaust), PID (intake), PID (exhaust), Errors D"},{"t":"GDI injectors","u":"modules/actuators/gdi.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → GDI injectors Injector Injector model (flow, pulse limits) and the PT2001 current profile. Curves 4 Injector opening delay vs differential pressure Injector delay battery correction Injector flow vs differential pressure Injector small-pulse correction Logger Live view in the application. Outputs Which output at which cylinder and the bank (rail, lambda), and a pulse check of each - engine stopped. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"PFI injectors","u":"modules/actuators/pfi.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → PFI injectors Injector 6 Port injectors on MC33810 OUT0..3 (two chips). Fuel share and end of injection - in «Fuel and mixture» -> «Injection». PFI injectors enabled PFI injector flow PFI reference pressure PFI pressure difference without sensor PFI minimum pulse PFI maximum pulse Curves 1 PFI opening delay vs battery Outputs Which output at which cylinder, and a pulse check of each - engine stopped. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Ignition coils","u":"modules/actuators/coils.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Ignition coils Dwell 6 Passive: charge time to the nominal current (start of learning); with igniter: the dwell from the datasheet. Dwell learning Dwell by battery Margin before spark Min dwell Max dwell Cycle fraction Current and spark 4 MC33810: nominal and fault current, chip dwell limit, weak spark threshold. Nominal current Fault current Chip max dwell Weak spark Outputs Which output at which cylinder, and a pulse check of each - engine stopped. Errors & limits Each coil of this engine: no spark, no current, overcurrent (MAXI), weak spark - passive coils (MC33810 sees current and spark)."},{"t":"HPFP","u":"modules/actuators/hpfp.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → HPFP Valve Quantity control valve: PT2001 current, hold pulse, closing delay. Mechanics Cam lobes, plunger, lobe position on the crank; cam lift picture with the closing point now. Curves 3 HPFP cam lift HPFP valve closing delay Leakage Controller 6 Regulator gain Integral gain Integral limit Integration band Rail volume Bulk modulus Pumps How many pumps (1 / 2) and where they deliver: pumps -> rails -> heads. Test Click the outputs 1..3 times - engine stopped. Errors & limits Live view in the application."},{"t":"Electronic throttle","u":"modules/actuators/etb.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Electronic throttle Throttle body 1 One actuator: position sensor(s) (input, calibration, stops learning) and motor (bridge, PWM, live trace, controller, auto-tuning). Throttle body 2 One actuator: position sensor(s) (input, calibration, stops learning) and motor (bridge, PWM, live trace, controller, auto-tuning). Spring 1 1 Duty holding the position against the spring (feed-forward); auto-tuning fills it. Throttle 1 spring feed-forward Spring 2 1 Duty holding the position against the spring (feed-forward); auto-tuning fills it. Throttle 2 spring feed-forward Stopped engine 4 Engine stopped longer than the delay - the bridge is switched off, the spring holds. The throttle position on a stopped engine and when cranking is set in Start. Throttle 1: release when stopped Throttle 1: release after Throttle 2: release when stopped Throttle 2: release after Test 1 Engine stopped: open to 0 / 25 / 50 / 75 / 100 % or by steps. Test 2 Engine stopped: open to 0 / 25 / 50 / 75 / 100 % or by steps. Errors 1 Live view in the application. Errors 2 Live view in the application."},{"t":"Wastegate pneumatic","u":"modules/actuators/wgValve.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Wastegate pneumatic Valve A Live view in the application. Valve B Live view in the application."},{"t":"Wastegate servo","u":"modules/actuators/wgServo.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Wastegate servo Servo A One actuator: position sensor(s) (input, calibration, stops learning) and motor (bridge, PWM, live trace, controller, auto-tuning). Servo B One actuator: position sensor(s) (input, calibration, stops learning) and motor (bridge, PWM, live trace, controller, auto-tuning). Spring A 1 Duty holding the position against the spring (feed-forward); auto-tuning fills it. Wastegate A spring feed-forward Spring B 1 Duty holding the position against the spring (feed-forward); auto-tuning fills it. Wastegate B spring feed-forward Stopped engine 4 Engine stopped longer than the delay - the bridge is switched off, the spring holds. The throttle position on a stopped engine and when cranking is set in Start. Wastegate 1: release when stopped Wastegate 1: release after Wastegate 2: release when stopped Wastegate 2: release after Test A Engine stopped: open to 0 / 25 / 50 / 75 / 100 % or by steps. Test B Engine stopped: open to 0 / 25 / 50 / 75 / 100 % or by steps. Errors A Live view in the application. Errors B Live view in the application."},{"t":"Cam phasers","u":"modules/actuators/phasers.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Cam phasers Intake A Valve output, live target / position / duty, valve check by hand, auto-tuning. Intake B Valve output, live target / position / duty, valve check by hand, auto-tuning. Exhaust A Valve output, live target / position / duty, valve check by hand, auto-tuning. Exhaust B Valve output, live target / position / duty, valve check by hand, auto-tuning. Intake regulator Rest stop, travel, PID and holding duty maps by rpm × oil temperature. Exhaust regulator Rest stop, travel, PID and holding duty maps by rpm × oil temperature. Holding (intake) PWM at which the cam stands still, by rpm × oil temperature: the start of the slow integrator (learned while running). The auto-tuning fills it. Holding (exhaust) PWM at which the cam stands still, by rpm × oil temperature: the start of the slow integrator (learned while running). The auto-tuning fills it. PID (intake) P, I, D by rpm × oil temperature (MED9 KFNWRPE / KFNWRSIE / KFNWRDE); the auto-tuning fills them. PID (exhaust) P, I, D by rpm × oil temperature (MED9 KFNWRPE / KFNWRSIE / KFNWRDE); the auto-tuning fills them. Errors Each valve: does not reach the target, no camshaft phase; reset"},{"t":"Power control","u":"modules/actuators/power.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Power control Switches Current and state of each switch; switch a bank off on a stopped engine. GDI limits 4 Working current range: below the lower - open load, above the upper - overload. Averaging, delays and retries are inside the firmware. Open-load current - GDI A Trip current - GDI A Open-load current - GDI B Trip current - GDI B Bank and internal limits 8 Working current range: below the lower - open load, above the upper - overload. Averaging, delays and retries are inside the firmware. Open-load current - bank A supply Trip current - bank A supply Open-load current - bank B supply Trip current - bank B supply Open-load current - internal supply Trip current - internal supply Open-load current - starter Trip current - starter"},{"t":"Oil pressure valve","u":"modules/actuators/oilValve.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Oil pressure valve Valve Output, kind, PWM, live pressure and duty, check by hand, auto-tuning. Feed-forward PWM by the target pressure at the nominal voltage - the regulator starts from it. Filled by the auto-tuning («Valve» tab). Regulator PI on the oil pressure sensor, PWM limits, battery correction. Errors Valve circuit and «does not hold the pressure»."},{"t":"Pumps & relays","u":"modules/actuators/relays.html","x":"What is in the application Actuators → Pumps & relays Pumps Which pump works, duty, current, failures; reset. Pump scheme 8 Feed scheme Pump mode Alternate main Pump PWM Prime time After stop Rotating above Cranking below Pump pressure 13 Schemes 2, 3: PWM holds the target pressure; sensor failed - pumps hold their current. Feed pressure target Max pressure Low pressure fault Low pressure time Settle time Kp Ki Min duty Safe current Safe min duty Current Ki Pump 1 curve valid Pump 2 curve valid Pump curves 5 Each curve is its own tab. Pump 1 duty Pump 2 duty Target correction Pump 1 pressure by current Pump 2 pressure by current Pump limits 4 Working current range: below the lower - open load, above the upper - overload. Averaging, delays and retries are inside the firmware. Open-load current - pump 1 Trip current - pump 1 Open-load current - pump 2 Trip current - pump 2 Lift pump 1 Low-pressure (lift) pump: its pressure is taken from the low fuel pressure sensor; without the sensor - this setting. Lift pressure without sensor Test Click the outputs 1..3 times - engine stopped."},{"t":"Start","u":"modules/start.html","x":"Start strategy Counting is by events and the dose is in mg. High-pressure start with low-pressure fallback, flood clear with the lever fully open, then extra fuel fades over cycles. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Engine modes The mode machine from off to running. Losing sync while running is not treated as a stop. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Now Tabs: Start state Description coming Conditions Tabs: Conditions Description coming Pressure Tabs: Rail pressure Description coming Start fuel Tabs: Fuel Description coming Start ignition Tabs: Ignition Description coming Air Tabs: Throttle and idle Description coming After start Tabs: Enrichment Description coming Warm-up Tabs: Lambda Description coming Adaptation Tabs: Start dose adaptation Description coming"},{"t":"Now","u":"modules/start/now.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Now Start state What the ECU is doing during the start right now and how the start works."},{"t":"Conditions","u":"modules/start/cond.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Conditions Conditions 9 End of start speed Starter protection Flood clear lever Burn detection Repeat start soak time Repeat start factor Hot start coolant Hot start air Hot start factor"},{"t":"Pressure","u":"modules/start/press.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Pressure Rail pressure 9 High-pressure start High-pressure start from High-pressure start up to Wait for pressure, revolutions Switch to low pressure Minimum pressure to inject Low-pressure start of injection Low-pressure maximum pulse First injection pressure"},{"t":"Start fuel","u":"modules/start/fuel.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Start fuel Fuel 5 Start dose Dose decay by injection number Dose by rail pressure Dose by cranking speed Start of injection at cranking"},{"t":"Start ignition","u":"modules/start/ign.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Start ignition Ignition 1 Start ignition"},{"t":"Air","u":"modules/start/air.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Air Throttle and idle 2 Throttle when stopped and cranking Idle speed"},{"t":"After start","u":"modules/start/post.html","x":"What is in the application Start → After start Enrichment 3 After-start enrichment After-start decay by cycles After-start time limit"},{"t":"Warm-up","u":"modules/start/warm.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Warm-up Lambda 1 Warm-up enrichment"},{"t":"Adaptation","u":"modules/start/adapt.html","x":"What is in the application Start → Adaptation Start dose adaptation 7 Start dose adaptation Adaptation step Adaptation minimum Adaptation maximum Slow start threshold Fast start threshold Learned start dose factor"},{"t":"Idle","u":"modules/idle.html","x":"Idle The propeller is always on the shaft, so idle requests torque rather than throttle percent. Functions are enabled in stages: controller, reserve and spark, loads, catch, adaptation. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Now and stages Tabs: Idle now Description coming Idle speed Tabs: Target and entry, Idle speed by coolant Description coming Regulator Tabs: Reserve and ignition, PID Description coming Loads and catch Tabs: Loads and catch Description coming Adaptation Tabs: Adaptation Description coming Faults Tabs: On faults Description coming"},{"t":"Now and stages","u":"modules/idle/now.html","x":"What is in the application Idle → Now and stages Idle now Live view in the application."},{"t":"Idle speed","u":"modules/idle/speed.html","x":"What is in the application Idle → Idle speed Target and entry Live view in the application. Idle speed by coolant 1 Idle speed"},{"t":"Regulator","u":"modules/idle/pid.html","x":"What is in the application Idle → Regulator Reserve and ignition Live view in the application. PID 1 Idle PID"},{"t":"Loads and catch","u":"modules/idle/loads.html","x":"What is in the application Idle → Loads and catch Loads and catch Live view in the application."},{"t":"Adaptation","u":"modules/idle/adapt.html","x":"What is in the application Idle → Adaptation Adaptation Live view in the application."},{"t":"Faults","u":"modules/idle/faults.html","x":"What is in the application Idle → Faults On faults Live view in the application."},{"t":"Boost","u":"modules/boost.html","x":"Module sections Boost target Tabs: Target Described Protection Tabs: Errors & limits Described"},{"t":"Boost target","u":"modules/boost/target.html","x":"Planned. This function is described in advance; part of it is not implemented yet. Purpose This module sets the target boost pressure from the torque path. The boost controller is not in the firmware yet , so the tab is marked as planned. How it works The common filling limit already works: protections and faults restrict air. Wastegate valves and servos are set up in the actuators module. After a MAP sensor failure, boost is allowed only from a new start. Torque how much air is needed ↓ Filling limit working now ↓ Boost target planned ↓ Wastegate actuator set up separately Setup 1 2 3 4 Engine → Configuration Open the Engine module Choose Configuration Set the turbos on the engine diagram Press Save to ECU Describe the boost system Engine → Configuration: turbos and wastegate type. Set up the actuator Actuators → pneumatic or servo wastegate. Check MAP The sensor must be calibrated for the required pressure range. Do not raise boost blindly Until the controller exists, the unit does not control pressure. Troubleshooting What you see Usually How to check The tab is empty The function is not implemented yet Watch for firmware updates Filling is limited A protection or fault is activ"},{"t":"Protection","u":"modules/boost/protect.html","x":"Planned. This function is described in advance; part of it is not implemented yet. Purpose Boost errors and limits will be here: overboost and sensor failure. This tab is planned. How it works Part of the protection already works through common mechanisms: a wastegate fault has its own torque and filling shares, and a MAP failure disables boost until a new start. Fault wastegate or MAP ↓ Torque and filling share working now ↓ Boost disabled after MAP failure, until a new start ↓ Overboost separate protection planned Setup Check the shares Torque & limits → Limits: wastegate fault. Check the MAP sensor Unplug it: the unit must detect the fault. Keep a mechanical safeguard Until overboost protection exists, do not rely on the unit alone. Troubleshooting What you see Usually How to check Engine limited after a MAP failure Boost is disabled until a new start Fix the fault and restart the engine What is in the application Boost → Protection Errors & limits Planned Overboost, sensor fault."},{"t":"Ignition","u":"modules/ignition.html","x":"Spark and knock Spark is calculated for the required torque. Knock in a cylinder window gives a fast retard with delayed recovery, the average retard feeds adaptation, and an octane factor blends the 92 and 100 maps. Retard causes no torque dip: air smoothly makes up the torque. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Spark timing Tabs: Maps Described Knock: detection Tabs: Setup, Noise map, Knock threshold Described Knock: retard Tabs: Setup, Retard Described Knock: fuel and fault Tabs: Fuel 92 / 100, Sensor fault Described"},{"t":"Spark timing","u":"modules/ignition/timing.html","x":"Purpose Four maps set the bounds of spark advance. The unit does not read the angle from a single table: it calculates the angle for the required torque and keeps it between the safe and the latest limits. How it works The two working maps are tuned on different fuels. An octane factor k blends between them, and knock retard is subtracted from the result. “100” map best fuel ↓ “92” map worst fuel, engine is safe ↓ Octane factor k 0 is the 92 map, 1 is the 100 map ↓ Spark for torque not earlier than the limit, not later than the latest SA = SA 100 − (1 − k) · (SA 100 − SA 92 ) − retard Spark, 100 map Spark, 92 map Octane factor k Knock retard Spark for this fuel - Final spark - Example calculation. Real angles come from maps over rpm and filling. Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Ignition → Spark timing Open the Ignition module Choose Spark timing Fill in the optimal angle Tune the 92 map on the worst fuel Tune the 100 map on the best fuel Set the latest angle Press Save to ECU Fill in MBT The optimal angle with the highest torque. The basis for spark efficiency. Tune the 92 map On the worst fuel you may get. The engine must survive full load on it. Tune the 100 map On the best fuel, watching for"},{"t":"Knock: detection","u":"modules/ignition/knock.html","x":"Purpose The unit listens to the knock sensors in a window after each cylinder's TDC and decides whether knock occurred. Each cylinder keeps its own background level, so a noisy cylinder does not trigger false detections. How it works The signal passes through one or two bands at the combustion chamber resonances. The knock feature is compared with the cylinder background: if the window is louder than the background by more than a set ratio, it is knock. Angle window default 10…60° after TDC, from 800 rpm ↓ Frequency bands chamber resonances, second band optional ↓ Feature RMS of band 1 + weight × RMS of band 2 ↓ Comparison feature / background above threshold knock if feature / background > threshold(n, rl) · k cyl · k transient Window vs background, times Threshold Retard step Step gain Decision - Retard per event - Firmware defaults: threshold 2.5, step 1.5°, gain 0.5. Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Ignition → Knock: detection Open the spectrograph: capture noise, place bands and window Open the Ignition module Choose Knock: detection The band frequency appears here after Apply to ECU Check the noise map over rpm Set the threshold slightly above the background Press Save to ECU Open the spe"},{"t":"Knock: retard","u":"modules/ignition/knockRetard.html","x":"Purpose What the unit does after hearing knock: it removes spark only on that cylinder, then carefully restores it. Torque does not dip, because air smoothly makes up the difference. How it works Each knock event adds a step to the cylinder's retard, and stronger knock means a larger step. After a quiet pause the angle returns at a set rate. The average retard slowly moves into an adaptation map over rpm and filling. Knock in a cylinder retard + step ↓ Limit default 10°, 6° at light load ↓ 0.5 s pause then recovery at 1 °/s ↓ Adaptation 16×16 map, up to 8° step = step(n) · (1 + gain · (ratio − threshold)) Window vs background, times Threshold Retard step Step gain Decision - Retard per event - Firmware defaults: threshold 2.5, step 1.5°, gain 0.5. Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Ignition → Knock: retard Choose Knock: retard Check the retard step over rpm Set the step gain Set the maximum retard Set the recovery rate Press Save to ECU Check the step Default 1.5° per event, gain 0.5. Set the maximum retard A map over rpm and filling. Set the recovery Fast recovery brings knock back, slow recovery leaves the engine short of torque. Check adaptation On a repeatable operating point, retard should mov"},{"t":"Knock: fuel and fault","u":"modules/ignition/knockFuel.html","x":"Purpose The unit estimates fuel quality and picks a point between the 92 and 100 maps. If the knock sensor fails, it moves to the safe map and limits the engine. How it works Factor k falls quickly when the average retard is high and rises slowly only after long quiet running under load. After refuelling it returns to the start value, because the new fuel is unknown. Start k = 0.5 after reset and refuelling ↓ Knock average retard above 3°: k − 0.1, at most every 5 s ↓ Quiet under load filling from 60%, 120 s: k + 0.02 ↓ Sensor fault k = 0, safety retard 6°, torque limit Spark, 100 map Spark, 92 map Octane factor k Knock retard Spark for this fuel - Final spark - Example calculation. Real angles come from maps over rpm and filling. Setup 1 2 3 4 5 6 Ignition → Knock: fuel and fault Choose Knock: fuel and fault Set the start value Check the refuel level threshold Enter 0 for 92 or 1 for 100 here manually Set the sensor fault reaction Press Save to ECU Set the start value The engine begins from it after refuelling. Check the level sensor Refuelling is detected by a level rise of 10% or more. Without the sensor there is no refuel reset. Set the rise rate Rising must be much slower than"},{"t":"Fuel & mixture","u":"modules/fuel.html","x":"Mixture and wideband correction Target lambda comes from a map with warm-up and exhaust protection corrections. Three independent wideband modes run: PI, fast cell correction and long-term adaptation. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Fuel and pulse Fuel per cycle is split between PFI and GDI (up to three pulses). Each part's time comes from the injector model at the actual pressure difference. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Lambda target Tabs: Maps Description coming Injection Tabs: Cycle scheme, Start of injection, GDI pulses, PFI Description coming Fuel pressure Tabs: Target, Rise Description coming Lambda control Tabs: Modes, Learned, Errors & limits Description coming Exhaust protection Tabs: Model, Errors & limits Description coming"},{"t":"Lambda target","u":"modules/fuel/target.html","x":"What is in the application Fuel & mixture → Lambda target Maps 1 Lambda target"},{"t":"Injection","u":"modules/fuel/inject.html","x":"What is in the application Fuel & mixture → Injection Cycle scheme Injection mode (GDI pulses 1/2/3, PFI on/off) and the 720° cycle with the pulses now. Start of injection 1 Start of injection GDI pulses 5 Second GDI pulse share Second GDI pulse start Third GDI pulse share Third GDI pulse start GDI split vs coolant PFI 3 PFI fuel share End of PFI injection PFI mix-in after start"},{"t":"Fuel pressure","u":"modules/fuel/pressure.html","x":"What is in the application Fuel & mixture → Fuel pressure Target 1 Rail pressure target Rise 1 Target ramp"},{"t":"Lambda control","u":"modules/fuel/closed.html","x":"What is in the application Fuel & mixture → Lambda control Modes 9 Lambda PID Heating from coolant Lambda settle time Fast point trim Fast trim: cycles in a cell Fast trim rate Fast trim limit Long-term adaptation Long-term learning rate Learned 2 Long-term lambda adaptation (F-RAM, kept). Reset: select all, type 1, Apply; or «ECU» page -> Adaptations -> Reset. Lambda adaptation A Lambda adaptation B Errors & limits 4 Lambda correction limit Fast trim limit Lambda adapt min Lambda adapt max"},{"t":"Exhaust protection","u":"modules/fuel/exh.html","x":"What is in the application Fuel & mixture → Exhaust protection Model 3 Exhaust temperature Late ignition heating Exhaust time constant Errors & limits 4 Exhaust limit Exhaust hysteresis Protection lambda Protection rate"},{"t":"Air (filling)","u":"modules/air.html","x":"From torque to throttle Torque becomes filling, then intake pressure, flow through the throttle and its angle: through physics rather than a lever-to-percent table. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Cylinder filling Tabs: Maps Description coming Throttle model Tabs: Area Description coming Fill calibration Tabs: Auto-calibration Description coming"},{"t":"Cylinder filling","u":"modules/air/fill.html","x":"What is in the application Air (filling) → Cylinder filling Maps 4 Filling slope, mg/kPa Residual pressure Exhaust cam correction Charge heating"},{"t":"Throttle model","u":"modules/air/throttle.html","x":"What is in the application Air (filling) → Throttle model Area 1 Throttle area"},{"t":"Fill calibration","u":"modules/air/autocal.html","x":"What is in the application Air (filling) → Fill calibration Auto-calibration Planned Live view in the application."},{"t":"Torque & limits","u":"modules/torque.html","x":"Torque structure The lever and idle controller request torque, limits take the minimum, and two paths deliver it: air slowly and spark quickly. That is why limits are smooth, with no cuts. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Torque reserve More air is held than needed and late spark absorbs the excess. When torque is needed at once, spark returns to optimum within one cycle, without waiting for the manifold to fill. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Stepped protections Each cause gives only a torque fraction from 0 to 1, and the strictest applies. The lowest step is a get-home mode, not zero. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Speed governor Experimental mode, off by default: the lever sets rpm and the unit finds the torque, with propeller feed-forward. Click to enlarge. How to read this diagram Diagram labels are in Russian for now; the description above explains the logic. Module sections Torque model Tabs: Maps Descr"},{"t":"Torque model","u":"modules/torque/model.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Torque model Maps 5 Optimal indicated torque Ignition efficiency Lambda efficiency Friction torque Pumping loss"},{"t":"Coordination","u":"modules/torque/coord.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Coordination Request 7 Lever torque request Limiter lead Air lead Soft zone Soft rise rate Rise rate Fall rate"},{"t":"Speed governor","u":"modules/torque/gov.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Speed governor Lever → speed Live view in the application."},{"t":"Limits","u":"modules/torque/limits.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Limits Errors & limits 4 Speed limit Limiter band Emergency cut Emergency margin"},{"t":"Limiters: now","u":"modules/torque/limNow.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Limiters: now Now Live view in the application."},{"t":"Protections (steps)","u":"modules/torque/protect.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Protections (steps) Coolant Live view in the application. Oil temperature Live view in the application. Exhaust Live view in the application. Gearbox Live view in the application. Oil pressure Live view in the application."},{"t":"Limiters: limits","u":"modules/torque/limMain.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Limiters: limits Limits 3 One torque limit and one fill limit (turbo and naturally aspirated). Every reason (faults below, knock) only gives a factor 0…1; several active - the strictest wins. A fault of level R1 or R2 turns on the factors of its path (R2 - plus an rpm limit ~2500), R3 - engine stop, R0 - code only. Max torque Max fill Knock: fill factor"},{"t":"Limiters: air and boost","u":"modules/torque/limAir.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Limiters: air and boost Factors 10 While the fault is active: torque and fill are limited to this share (0…1) of the central limits. Several - the strictest. Throttle: torque share Throttle: fill share Throttle sensor: torque share Throttle sensor: fill share Lever: torque share Lever: fill share MAP sensor: torque share MAP sensor: fill share Wastegate: torque share Wastegate: fill share"},{"t":"Limiters: fuel","u":"modules/torque/limFuel.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Limiters: fuel Factors 4 While the fault is active: torque and fill are limited to this share (0…1) of the central limits. Several - the strictest. Rail sensor: torque share Rail sensor: fill share Rail pressure: torque share Rail pressure: fill share"},{"t":"Limiters: engine","u":"modules/torque/limEngine.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Limiters: engine Factors 10 While the fault is active: torque and fill are limited to this share (0…1) of the central limits. Several - the strictest. Coolant temp.: torque share Coolant temp.: fill share Air temp.: torque share Air temp.: fill share Sync: torque share Sync: fill share Crank sensor: torque share Crank sensor: fill share Knock sensor: torque share Knock sensor: fill share"},{"t":"Limiters: protections and survival","u":"modules/torque/limSurvive.html","x":"What is in the application Torque & limits → Limiters: protections and survival Factors 23 While the fault is active: torque and fill are limited to this share (0…1). The step protections lower the torque themselves (section «Protections»); these factors are what the fault of that path adds on top. Changed only by a person. Torque limit: oil pressure Fill limit: oil pressure Torque limit: coolant overheat Fill limit: coolant overheat Torque limit: oil overheat Fill limit: oil overheat Torque limit: exhaust overheat Fill limit: exhaust overheat Torque limit: gearbox overheat Fill limit: gearbox overheat Torque limit: oil pressure sensor Fill limit: oil pressure sensor Torque limit: oil temperature sensor Fill limit: oil temperature sensor Torque limit: low battery voltage Fill limit: low battery voltage Torque limit: input chip silent Fill limit: input chip silent Torque limit: crank sensor lost (running on the camshaft) Fill limit: crank sensor lost (running on the camshaft) Torque limit: engine health Fill limit: engine health «Go home» engine speed limit"},{"t":"Engine health","u":"modules/health.html","x":"Module sections Now Tabs: Signs Description coming Fuel use and range Tabs: Now Description coming Tolerances Tabs: Tolerances Description coming Propeller Tabs: Learning Description coming"},{"t":"Now","u":"modules/health/now.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Now Signs Live view in the application."},{"t":"Fuel use and range","u":"modules/health/eco.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Fuel use and range Now Live view in the application."},{"t":"Tolerances","u":"modules/health/tol.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Tolerances Tolerances 8 How far a sign may go from its normal value before the ECU marks it. A sign that stays outside for the confirmation time sets the «Engine health» code (level and limits - «Torque & limits»). Tolerance: propeller load Tolerance: cylinder roughness Tolerance: intake pressure vs model Tolerance: rail pressure Tolerance: fuel correction Tolerance: cylinders at the shortest pulse Tolerance: sync losses Health: confirmation time"},{"t":"Propeller","u":"modules/health/prop.html","x":"What is in the application Engine health → Propeller Learning 5 The ECU learns the propeller factor k (torque = k·n²) on steady running and then watches how the load differs from it. Here - when the running counts as steady and how slowly k is learned. Propeller: minimum rpm Propeller: steady rpm rate Propeller: steady time Propeller: learning time Propeller: minimum torque"},{"t":"CAN","u":"modules/can.html","x":"Module sections Buses Tabs: CAN1 / CAN2 Description coming Dash Tabs: Dash 6.8 Description coming OBD-II and NMEA 2000 Tabs: Outside Description coming"},{"t":"Buses","u":"modules/can/buses.html","x":"What is in the application CAN → Buses CAN1 / CAN2 Live view in the application."},{"t":"Dash","u":"modules/can/dash.html","x":"What is in the application CAN → Dash Dash 6.8 Live view in the application."},{"t":"OBD-II and NMEA 2000","u":"modules/can/out.html","x":"What is in the application CAN → OBD-II and NMEA 2000 Outside Live view in the application."},{"t":"Tools","u":"tools/index.html","x":"Trigger wheel Time per tooth and degree at different speeds. Fuel and pulse Air, fuel, pulse length, injector duty. Temperature sensor NTC sensor resistance and voltage."},{"t":"Trigger wheel","u":"tools/trigger.html","x":"Trigger wheel Change the wheel and speed to see how much time the unit has per tooth and which stroke cylinder 1 is on. Wheel 60-2 36-1 12-1 Engine speed Cycle angle Pause One tooth - Gap - One degree - Cycle, 720° - Rotation is slowed down. Numbers are for the selected speed."},{"t":"Fuel and pulse","u":"tools/fuel.html","x":"Fuel and injector pulse An estimate for sizing injectors and checking maps. Move the values and see what runs out first. Displacement Cylinders Engine speed Intake pressure Air Volumetric efficiency Lambda Injector at 100 bar Rail pressure Opening delay Air per cycle - Fuel per cycle - Pulse - Pulse angle - Duty - A simplified estimate for one pulse per cycle, gasoline (14.7). For tuning, take values from the application."},{"t":"Temperature sensor","u":"tools/ntc.html","x":"How it works The sensing element is an NTC thermistor: resistance falls as temperature rises. The sensor is connected to ground and forms a voltage divider with a 10 kΩ reference resistor from 5 V. Temperature the sensing element heats up ↓ Resistance falls along the NTC curve ↓ Input voltage divider with the reference resistor ↓ The unit calculates degrees using the sensor calibration Sensor characteristic Temperature Resistance at 25 °C β coefficient Sensor resistance - Input voltage - State - Example for a typical sensor. Take exact values from your sensor's datasheet."}];
